The only bridge that leads to the district due to a typhoon and heavy rain is drained Residents have tripled the stockpile of food <Fukushima Prefecture's Yamatsuri-cho> (Fukushima TV) - Yahoo! News
Koro Matsumoto in the Takajihara area of Yatsuri Town, Fukushima Prefecture.
The typhoon in eastern Japan had a significant impact on the lives of eight people in three households.
Mr. Koro Matsumoto: "At the beginning, I felt like 'seriously.'
Kojihara Bridge, the only road that leads to the Kojihara area, was swept away by the typhoon in eastern Japan, and thirty-one residents of the district became isolated.
Residents who were forced to cut off the water continued to carry the necessary supplies using JR's iron bridge, which is usually not allowed to pass.
Mr. Yoshiro Matsumoto: "Usually a car can come to the garden, but when you get out of the car over there, you hold it in your hand, or walk over the iron bridge on your back, so you can feel grateful for transportation and cars."
More than two years have passed since the disaster, but Mr. Matsumoto continues to take measures based on the lessons learned.
Koro Matsumoto "I think there are about ten cases.
For the time being, I would like to somehow secure 2-3 days of food that can only be used for non-emergency meals, such as canned goods and cup noodles.
Misako MATSUMOTO: "I didn't know when a disaster would happen to me, it's not someone else's business, so I felt that it's better to stockpile a small amount of money."
In order not to forget the lesson, the town started its own initiative in 2021.
Mr. Ryuichi Takahashi, Section Manager of the Education Division of Yatsuri-cho, said, "It has been decided in the form of 'Yamatsuri-cho Disaster Prevention Day', and there is also an announcement that October 12th should be a day to think about disaster preparedness for disaster prevention after next year.
I think there has been an increase in the awareness that unexpected things can happen more than ever before.”
