Pay attention when buying a second home!
What are the pitfalls of the "building situation survey system" (Diamond Online) - Yahoo! News
In order to revitalize the second-hand housing market The newly established "building situation survey" system The second-hand housing business is doing well.
The prices of detached houses and condominiums are rising, and the renovation market is also on an expanding trend.
While the coronavirus pandemic has increased the desire to own a home, it may also be influenced by the fact that the popularity is concentrated in properties represented by the words such as "in front of the station", "near station", "large-scale" and "tower", which are soaring, and the drastic decrease in the number of newly-built properties that are relatively cheap has drastically decreased.
[See the image in this article] However, compared to overseas, the share of existing (second-hand) housing distribution in Japan is still at a low level.
Therefore, the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism (Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism (MLIT (MLIT) revised the Real Estate Transaction Business Act in 2018 to investigate the status of buildings with the aim of dealing with safe and secure second-hand houses, called so-called inspection (https://www.mlit.go.jp//common/001219900.pdf).
This is a system in which an architect who is familiar with housing checks the current situation, including the deterioration of the property and the condition of the building, summarizes it in a report and presents it to the buyer.
The Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism explains that the introduction of inspections has benefits for both buyers and sellers.
This is because the seller is able to evade post-delivery claims by conducting a property survey before the sale transaction, which gives the buyer a sense of security.
This will also lead to the differentiation of competing properties.
On the other hand, on the buyer's side, it is a system that can grasp the state of the second-hand house scheduled to be purchased, and it is a system that can be purchased convincingly.
However, in the current system of the current system, it is mainly the seller's side who has the initiative to conduct a survey of the building situation.
In addition, I would also like to note that only the announcement of the system of inspection has become obligatory.
In other words, the real estate agent in charge of brokerage has an obligation to explain, “In the case of property intermediation, we can introduce an architecture who investigates the status of buildings” or “I can’t”, but the implementation itself is not obligatory.
On the other hand, a similar system has already been put in place in the United States and the United Kingdom, where second-hand markets are booming, similar systems have already been put in place.
In the preliminary stage of the house sale, the purchaser asks an expert called "inspector" or a "building surveyor" to conduct a building survey.
For example, in the U.S., 70 to 90 percent of second-hand residential transactions, depending on the state.
In terms of "buyers" taking the initiative to conduct inspection, the Japanese-style inspection of "sellers" is greatly different.
The seller looks at the condition of the building a little better and tries to sell it as high as much as possible and as soon as possible.
On the other hand, purchasers would like to study the contents of the survey and make appropriate repairs and live with peace of mind.
Even if it is the same inspection, there is a misalignment in the goals and feelings of both parties.
For this reason, the following points have been pointed out as a worrying risk for the Japanese system.
● The scope of the investigation of seller-led inspections established by the government shall be kept to a minimum.
Because it is not a detailed investigation, there is a risk of overlooking a really important problem ● For an inspection company, it can be said that the intermediary company (seller) that introduces the project in charge is a customer.
Therefore, there is a possibility that an "extraordinary" inspection, such as not reporting unfavorable findings to the seller
