Was Ginza an island?
Five miscellaneous schools that you can talk while wearing silver (LEON.JP) - Yahoo! News
“This used to be a peninsula.
In the evidence of that, it is unique to Ginza that you can perform a little miscellaneous study.
I would like to introduce the trivia of Ginza that you will surely want to talk to someone who is interesting to know.
Let's go over Ginza! ?
infiltrate the kinza for the first time.
If you are walking in Ginza, you will find quirky place names, mysterious stone monuments, and a large number of shrines, as well as remnants of the history carved out of the land of Ginza.
When I was walking around Ginza with her, I unexpectedly came to the silence and said, "This used to be a peninsula.
Has Ginza ever been a peninsula?
"Ginza" in Chuo-ku, Tokyo is synonymous with the downtown area that created "XX Ginza" in various parts of Japan.
In the first place, "Ginza" is a foundry of silver coins, and in the 1612s, the Ginza government office, which had been located in present-day Shizuoka City, was moved to Edo.
At that time, it was referred to as 'new money exchange town,' but when it was moved to Kyugarish-cho, it came to be commonly called 'Ginza.'
By the way, it is said that the vicinity of Ginza used to be a " peninsula" protruding into the sea.
Until around the Edo period, from Hibiya to Marunouchi, co-embeins entered, and the peninsula called 'Edo-no-Emae Island' stretched from Nihonbashi to Ginza.
The tip of the peninsula corresponds to present-day Ginza.
When Ieyasu TOKUGAWA established a bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) in the Edo period (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun), he reclaimed coves and promoted the development of the Kyobashi area.
In addition, Ieyasu built a terrestrial area and built a moat-warrior to develop a ferry.
He cut off the soil and Mt. Kanda that had shaved off the moat and buried the cove.
In front of the Tiffany Ginza main store (Ginza 2-7, Chuo-ku), there is a stone monument of "the birthplace of Ginza" that shows the site of the Ginza government office, so why don't you look for it if you are interested.
"Pilgrimage to sacred places" in Ginza known to those in the know
In Ginza, "Ginza Hatcho-jinja Shrine Meguri" is called "Ginza Hatcho-jinja Shrine Tour", a "pilgrimage to sacred places" that is known only to those in the know, is held every year
"Kabuki Inari-jinja Shrine" is located in front of the Kabuki-za Theater.
The shrine, which stands on the right edge of the Kabuki-za Theater, is often visited by many people who visit before and after a performance.
On the other hand, "Ryuko Fudo-son" is on the rooftop of Ginza Matsuya, and "Ginza success Jizo-son" is located on the 9th floor of Ginza Mitsukoshi.
"Hōdō Inari-jinja Shrine", "Azuma Inari-jinja Shrine", "Azuma Inari-jinja Shrine" and "Toyiwa Inari-jinja Shrine" are located in the back of the alley and are not conspicuously conspicuous, but "Toyiwa Inari-jinja Shrine" is known for revered by Kabuki-famous actor Uzaemon Ichimura, and "Succissant Inari Shrine" at the 1st floor entrance is not open to the public except when visiting Hatcho-jinja Shrine.
There are also places that enshrine Fudo Myoo and Jizo Bodhisattva (Bodhisattva Ksitigarbha), but most shrines enshrine Inari.
"Inari-sha Shrine", where there are many farmhouses where there are many farmhouses, was often built in front of the main shrine building because it is said that there is a benefit of "five grain rich harvests" where there are many farmers.
In large cities such as Kyoto and the Edo period, it came to be said to be beneficial for prosperous business, and even in Ginza, Japan's busiest shopping district, religious beliefs began to spread.
