At Gokoku-jinja Shrine (Yamagata City), which is preparing for the New Year, a large votive tablet of "Tora" (Tora), the zodiac sign of the coming year, appeared.
On the 11th, students of Tohoku University of Arts and Technology, who designed it, visited the shrine and held a memorial service in front of the gods.
For the past two years, the shrine has been commissioned by Geigi University students for the original painting of Ema.
This time, three-year-old Yui Tsuchiya Yui (22-year-old Tsuchiya Yui) was selected from among the nine members of the Japanese painting course in the art department taught by Professor Toshiaki Suenaga.
The octopus is 3.3 metres wide and the center is about 1.8 metres high.
Tsuchiya drew it with "an image where cherry blossoms gently push their backs as they step into a new year."
The figure of the tiger who stopped and looked at the cherry blossoms said, "Even in a world that changes violently, you can enjoy the beauty of nature."
Tsuchiya's design is also depicted on votive votive votively distributed in general.
Kenichi Chiba, a 44-year-old from Gonnegi, said, "I think it's going to help people overcome difficulties."
