Let's think about "Tamano who wants to continue to live" To the proposal Four high school students in the city Workshop: Sanyo Shimbun Digital|SANDIGI
What kind of town is Tamano in the 2030s?
A student representative from four high schools in Tamano City is working on a joint workshop to think about "Tamano who wants to continue to live".
As a part of a research and policy proposal project of the Tamano SDGs Miraizukuri Center, an interim support organization, we will summarize the voices of young people and make recommendations to the mayor and others.
“When are you having fun?”
In the facilitator's questions, the student answers aloud, such as "when you get excited with a lot of people" and "when you hit the drums."
It was held for the first time at Hinode Fureai Hall on November 13th.
Eighteen high school high school students and people from the local community talked about "an interesting town", "a livable town", and "a town where you can feel the meaning of life" using the "world cafe" method of "world cafe" in which the theme is decided.
Each idea is written down on a sticky note and placed on imitation paper to organize it.
“A town with a large number of young people seems to be interesting,” “Transportation such as trains and buses is essential for livability.”
Let’s see if there’s anything in common and let’s talk more.
“I thought Tamano was boring, but being able to cycle on the seaside is fascinating,” said a third-year student at Tamano Shoko High School, 18.
"There are few shops and transportation is inconvenient, but it's rich in nature is a good place," said an 18-year-old third-year student at Binnan High School, and by talking with adults, he seemed to have noticed not only the challenges of the region but also the charm.
For the second time in December, only high school students gathered to sort out the contents of the proposal.
In February of next year, we plan to invite the mayor and the head of the education department to make an announcement.
"We hope that it will foster local love for high school students as well as an opportunity for young people to be actively involved in community development," said Rie Higashi, president of the center.
