Unusual warmth and strong cold air effects in the U.S. (Weather News) - Yahoo! News
There were a number of tornadoes, mainly in the southern part of the United States, from the 10th (Friday) night to the 11th (Saturday).
During this period, it seems that cumulonimbus clouds developed rapidly due to very unstable atmospheric conditions due to the influence of strong cold air moving south where it was a record-breaking warmth.
In the southern part of the United States, warm air flowed in from the southwest, resulting in record-high temperatures.
In Houston, Texas, the maximum temperature on the 9th (Thursday) and the 10th (Friday) of local time has risen to close to 30oC.
In such a record-breaking warmth, a strong cold air below -30°C was moving southward around 5500m in the sky, so a cumulonimbus cloud quickly developed in a way that was in line with the front.
A series of tornadoes are believed to have occurred in the surrounding area.
The southern part of the U.S. is originally an area where tornadoes are prone, but there are not many cases of heavy damage at this time from early summer to late summer.
The tornado warning, which was issued in a single day during the winter season, reached close to 150 times and was a record-breaking number.
The National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration has reported more than four hundred tornadoes, gusty winds, etc. on the 10th (Friday) local time.
According to this, it can be seen that there are two concentrated areas: the southern line from Kentucky to Texas and the northern line including Illinois.
In Kentucky, the EF scale (improved Fujita scale), which shows the strength of a tornado, reached EF3, which is the third from the strongest of the six stages, and it is thought that a gust of gusts of around 70m/s blew instantaneously.
There is a possibility that it will be judged to be EF4 to EF5 according to future investigations.
In Japan, JEF (Japanese improved Fujita scale) is used according to the situation of Japanese buildings based on the improved Fujita scale.
JEF3, which is equivalent to a tornado in the United States this time, is likely to occur in Japan, and it is at the level where wooden houses collaps.
