A long road for a family that rooted environmental protection activities on the frontier of China (JBpress) - Yahoo! News
(Yuki Kato: Hong Kong company Find Asia Corporate Consultative) China, which has a large land area, is also important in terms of biodiversity, and there are the world's largest types of mammals and the world's three types of plants.
Recognizing the value of biodiversity in some local governments, there are also areas that promote not only environmental protection but also the activities of environmental activists.
Located in south-western part of the country in southern China, Xispanna in Yunnan Province is also one such region.
His younger sister, Lee, is currently active as an actress. Sisappanna is an area bordered by both Laos and Myanmar, and is home to many ethnic ethnic minorities, such as the "Thai (Thailand) people" who share their roots with the Thai people of the Kingdom of Thailand.
The headwaters of the Mekong River, which flows through Southeast Asian countries.
Sisappanna-Thai (West Shuangzhuang-ban-no-Pong) Autonomous Region The area of Sisappanna is only 0.2 percent of the whole of China, but there are about twenty-five percent types of wild animals and twenty percent kinds of plants.
This time, we interviewed Mr. Lee, who is engaged in environmental protection activities in Sisappanna, who is engaged in environmental protection activities in Sisappanna.
I started environmental protection activities together with my German husband ――I heard that it’s been twenty years since I started environmental protection activities.
What kind of trigger was it?
Mr. Lee Our environmental protection activity "Tenfu" has been active since the 2000s.
This is my husband Josef Margraf.
My husband is from Germany and came to Sisapanna for a joint science project between the United Nations and China.
It was the only tropical rainforest region in China and boasted one of the leading biodiversity.
However, on the other hand, economic development was lagging behind.
Therefore, in order to take up the roots of industry in this area, large-scale deforestation of the former rainforest progressed in order to produce rubber, which was lacking in China.
My husband was sent here from Germany for the restoration of natural resources and the study of biological resources.
When I was a journalist, I met and got married.
Were you not interested in the protection of nature before that?
Mr. Lee Although I am from Sisapanna, I had no interest in the importance of tropical rainforests or the meaning of biodiversity.
Therefore, I decided to promote the project with my husband without having that knowledge.
