South Korea's hydrogen technology patents, one-fifth of China's "destible to implement the hydrogen economic roadmap" (Chuo Nippo) - Yahoo!
The South Korean government announced a hydrogen economic roadmap in 2019.
Sixty-five thousand fuel-fed cell vehicles will be introduced (cumulatively) by 2022, and 310 hydrogen stations will be operated throughout the country.
The target hydrogen price was 6000won (approximately 577 yen) per kilo.
However, fuel cell-powered vehicles are only 27 per cent of the target (approximately 17000 vehicles) on a November-based basis in November this year.
The schedule for disseminating hydrogen stations in the core infrastructure is also 38% (approximately 117places), and the hydrogen price per kilometre does not differ significantly from when the roadmap was announced.
It turned out that the implementation status of the government's hydrogen economic roadmap is well below the target.
On the 12th, the National Federation of Economies (Zenkei-ren) released a report "Hydrogen economic ecosystem current situation and policy direction".
According to the report, the core components and materials of the hydrogen industry depend mostly on the United States and Japan.
Carbon fiber, which is the core material of fuel cell vehicles, is supplied by Toray (Japan), KYOCERA (Japan) for platinum catalysen (cylinthings), Dupont (U.S.), Gore (U.S.), and 3M (U.S.).
The number of hydrogen technology patents in the six leading countries in the global hydrogen market (China, the United States, the European Union, Japan, South Korea, and Germany) is increasing on an average of 14% per a year.
South Korea ranks fifth in the world in terms of the number of patents in the hydrogen production and fuel cell fields (cumulonimposed in 2014-2020).
“In effect, six countries accounted for the majority of the world’s hydrogen market, and in fact it means that it is the lowest among competitive countries.”
The country-by-national patent rankings are followed by China, the United States, the EU, and Japan.
The gap has widened since China surpassed the U.S. in first place.
The number of patents registered in 2020 came in fourth place, surpassing Japan's (974 cases) with 1033 cases in South Korea (974cases), compared to 22% in China (4721cases).
"Hydrogen technology should be designated as a 'national strategic technology,' which has a large range of capital investment and R&D expenses tax credits," Zen Keiren said.
