The U.S., aid weapons and ammunition arrive in Ukraine, also engaged in troop training (CNN.co.jp) - Yahoo!
(CNN) -- A U.S. State Department official reported on Tuesday that equipment, including small firearms and ammunition, had arrived in Ukraine under a security aid program approved by President Biden.
At the beginning of the new year, military aid is equivalent to sixty million dollars, and the last piece of equipment, including four radars that identify the launch site of mortars, will be transported to Ukraine at the beginning of the year.
According to U.S. Department of Defense spokesman Kirby, these aids also included lethal weapons, such as anti-tank "Javerin".
The purpose of military support is to strengthen Ukraine's national defense capabilities in response to the Russian army's troop build-up around Ukraine's border.
The U.S. has become more wary of the moves of Russian forces and has consulted with members of the North-Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) to develop a response in the event of a Russian invasion of Ukraine.
U.S. President Volodymyr President Zelensky approved weapons aid when Ukrainian President Zelensky visited the U.S. White House.
U.S. military assistance to Ukraine reached nearly $450 million this year.
After Russia forcibly annexed Ukraine’s Crimean Peninsula, the total amount of aid has risen to more than $25bn.
The U.S. military is also involved in the training mission of the Ukrainian Armed Forces.
Last month, it dispatched a U.S. state trooper in Florida in accordance with a multi-pronged joint Ukrainian-sponsorial training program.
The U.S. Special Operations Force in Europe has also played a major role in training and advising Ukrainian special forces, according to a statement from the Pentagon.
The number of U.S. special forces operating in Ukraine has not been identified as a secret in the conduct of the operation.
On the 7th of this month, President Biden had an online meeting with Russian President Vladimir Putin, but there were also tense exchanges in the situation in Ukraine.
In the event of an invasion, the U.S. is also poised to impose severe economic sanctions.
