"Re-examination" of Inheritance and gift tax "reconsidered" that makes lifetime gifts "forbidden", directly hit Akira Amari and the LDP's former tax commission chairman!
The first special feature in the December 18th issue of "Weekly Diamond" is "Talking with your family during the year-end and New Year holidays!
It’s a life-or-death gift-and-grain inheritance.”
In the midst of the discussion of the integration of inheritance tax and gift tax, it was postponed in the 2022 tax reform outline, which we were concerned about, but in the near future, there is a great possibility that lifetime gifts, which are the royal road of inheritance tax measures, will become "prohibited".
How will the rules of inheritance change?
Along with a direct hit to the LDP's former tax-choice-choice-choice-choice-president Akira Amari-no-Mitsui (LDP) members of the House of Amari-no-House of Representatives, we will teach you everything from gift-making to tax-saving techniques.
Hiroaki Miyahara (Diamond Editorial Department )
We will proceed with full-blown studies toward the construction of a tax system that is neutral to the timing of asset transfers, from the perspective of curating inheritance tax and gift tax in a more integrated manner."
The ruling party announced last year's tax-imension plan, which was announced last year, caused a great deal of turbulence not only to wealthy people but also to the middle class.
This is because it means to completely change the rules of inheritance and to increase the inheritance tax by making the "lifetime gift" which is the royal road of inheritance tax measures as "prohibited".
The iron rule of tax-saving measures for highly progressive inheritance tax is to "reduce taxable assets."
Its strongest trump card, lifetime gifts, have been widely used not only by property owners, but also by company employees who have homes in the three major metropolitan areas and incur inheritance tax on a small amount due to having homes in the three major metropolitan areas.
In the midst of that, the discussion of "inheritance tax and gift tax integration" came down.
In the FY22 tax reform outline announced on December 10th, which had been announced on December 10th that there were concerns about the fastest introduction, the policy aimed at integration with a more in-depth wording was reiterated, such as “Reconsidering the current inheritance settlement taxation system and the calendar-year gifting system” and “Since the tax-exempt measure of gift tax is a system that does not require any tax burden on the transfer of assets within the family within the limit within the limit, we will constantly review it.”
Akira Amari, the former chairman of the LDP's tax investigation committee, was interviewed by this editorial department.
"The most important thing is to lose the advantages and disadvantages due to the timing of the transfer of the property, and to keep the tax burden the same at any time.
We are currently simulating what kind of problems (by the integration) will come out.”
Even if this is not the case, there is no doubt that in the near future, there is no doubt that there will be a big rudder in the direction of invalidating lifetime gifts as a tax-saving measure.
So, how exactly will the inheritance and gift tax rules change?
