The sponge.
No matter how often you change, it’s a sponge that you probably don’t change as often as it’s recommended.
According to last year’s research, kitchen sponges can breed a lot of bacteria, even if they are sterilized in the microwave every day (it’s still a good recommendation to sterilize them in a microwave).
The German researchers who did the study recommend replacing the sponge once a week to keep it clean.
The cutting board.
The U.S. Department of Agriculture has pointed out that cutting boards should be replaced if it begins to scrape off, scratches and grooves.
This is because once scratches and grooves progress, it becomes difficult to keep it clean, and bacteria grow.
Don’t forget to frequently disinfect one-gallon (approximately 3.8 liters) with a tablespoon-dissolve solution of unscented liquid bleach.
Immerse the surface of the cutting board in the liquid and place for a couple of minutes.
Rinse it with clean water, dry it naturally or lightly with a clean paper towel.
